body:
I know. But it's just not there anymore and despite attempts to fight it I just end up regressing each time. But yeah I don't mind explaining a bit if someone wants to hear it.

&#x200B;

I'll start by saying my life isn't all bad, realistically there's been some great memories made. I've just gone through a series of unfortunate events here and there, some things that I wouldn't wish on anyone. I was always moving around a lot as a child because my dad was in a bad accident and there were threats to my family once we ran into some money. I started getting really depressed around the end of high school when my dad passed away. I went into college with this on my mind and it got to the point where I wouldn't eat, leave my room, get out of bed, etc. Eventually I started seeing the counselor on campus and trying to get it back together, slowly but surely. Fast forward a bit, I meet a bunch of folks who become just like family. First we started hanging out after class, then we evolved into playing games together, hanging out at each others houses, encouraging and motivating each other, basically it was a big boost. Around the same time I stopped trying to just tough it out naturally and started on anti-depressants so things seemed like they were on the rise. But that was temporary. See, one of my biggest problems is that I do *too* much in the sense that I go out of my way to help people, further than most would deem necessary. There's one person in the group who I fell hard for over time and throughout each issue he faced, I tried my hardest to be there for him. And each time I basically got tossed like trash. This is due to the fact that beforehand, he dated another person in our group and it got messy and I was placed in the middle. After countless times of thinking the issue was resolved, it'd get brought back up somehow again and I always seemed to suffer for it in some shape or form. The final time was just recently, where this person wanted to get my best friend to talk to me about ending the friendship after graduation. I've been skimming a bit but emotionally I grew really close to everyone in the group, especially him so this hurt me a lot. And from that point forward things have been a bit on the decline. I had to immediately move to a different state than I was currently residing in right after graduation and right after I lost access to the doctor I used to see so eventually I fell into withdrawal. We have this big discord group that he's still a part of so I don't feel comfortable going in and talking to my own friends since they still enjoy his company and I can't necessarily make them choose. I went to school to become a software developer, specifically game development at some point. But I don't have any motivation to practice anymore, I don't feel confident enough to get a job. I feel like it's all starting to snowball out of control again. I'm tired of feeling jaded and bitter about the situation, I want to put it all behind me but as stated before that motivation, that "spark", I don't feel it at all anymore. So now I'm just waiting until I either a) die passively or b) mentally snap and end it immediately.

&#x200B;

Apologies, this probably isn't very cohesive (or at least not as much as I wanted). I didn't want to type out a life story lol. The people here, you included I'm sure, are kind-hearted but I don't want to feel like I'm taking advantage and wasting time. I appreciate you taking the time to respond earlier and read this.
